UPDATE artists SET bio_en = 'Milly, Jocelyn y Los Vecinos was a Dominican merengue orchestra formed by four siblings of the Quezada Borbón family in Washington Heights, Manhattan, in 1975, one of the first female-fronted merengue acts and a formative outlet for Milly Quezada, later known as La Reina del Merengue.

**Washington Heights**

The family had left Santo Domingo for New York during the Dominican civil war of the mid-1960s, and the group grew out of street singing among the siblings in the growing Dominican neighbourhood of Washington Heights. The oldest brother, Rafael Quezada, directed and arranged for the group; Milly Quezada and her sister Jocelyn sang lead, with another brother, Martín, also in the lineup. The band first performed as Milly y Los Vecinos before taking the fuller name once Jocelyn was singing lead alongside her sister.

**A woman-led merengue**

Through the late 1970s and into the 1980s and 1990s the group built a following among the Dominican diaspora with songs such as «La Guacherna» and «Volvió Juanita» — both written by the Colombian composer Esthercita Forero, of Barranquilla — «Tengo» and «Lo mío es mío / Amanecemos parrandeando», written by the Colombian musician Israel Romero. Their records include «Ahora es..!» (1989) and «7+1 = Vecinos» (1990), the latter opening with «La Reina» and including «Viejo Luis», a bachata. In 1990 the group says it played a Presidential Inaugural Gala for George H. W. Bush in Washington, D.C., and it toured Japan, giving some of the first live Dominican merengue performances there, in Tokyo, Nagasaki and Nagoya.

**Legacy**

The group’s run effectively ended when Milly Quezada launched her solo career in 1997, after the 1996 death of her husband and manager Rafael Vázquez. Milly, Jocelyn y Los Vecinos is remembered as one of the first orchestras to put a woman at the front of Dominican merengue, at a time when the genre’s bandstands were almost entirely male.' WHERE slug = 'milly-jocelyn-y-los-vecinos';

